DRBD 9.3.2, HEAD a46cbd9 (linux/drbd_config.h:14 `#define REL_VERSION "9.3.2"`). Both source trees byte-identical at the cited ranges.

CONNECT PATH — drbd_sync_handshake() in /tmp/drbdsrc/drbd/drbd_receiver.c:
  5401  /* Evaluate the original strategy,
  5402   * before it is re-mapped by additional configuration below.
  5403   */
  5404  need_full_sync_after_split_brain = (strategy == SPLIT_BRAIN_DISCONNECT);
  5406  if (strategy == SPLIT_BRAIN_AUTO_RECOVER || (strategy == SPLIT_BRAIN_DISCONNECT && always_asbp)) {
  ...   (5417-5429: drbd_asb_recover_{0,1,2}p remap strategy -> SYNC_{SOURCE,TARGET}_USE_BITMAP)
  5434      if (need_full_sync_after_split_brain) {
  5439          drbd_warn(... "Doing a full sync, since UUIDs where ambiguous.\n");
  5441          strategy = strategy_descriptor(strategy).full_sync_equivalent;   // -> *_SET_BITMAP (full)
The claim's line 5404 (connect) is an EXACT match. For an AUTO_RECOVER case, strategy==SPLIT_BRAIN_AUTO_RECOVER, so line 5404 sets the flag FALSE; the block at 5406 is still entered (AUTO_RECOVER condition), the asb_recover_*p call remaps to SYNC_*_USE_BITMAP, and at 5434 the FALSE flag skips 5435-5441 — the USE_BITMAP (incremental) result is kept. Confirmed.

ATTACH PATH — drbd_attach_handshake() at :5232 (exact match): `need_full_sync_after_split_brain = (strategy == SPLIT_BRAIN_DISCONNECT);`, with the identical promotion gated at 5257-5266. Same behavior.

WHY AUTO_RECOVER ==> incremental is semantically correct: drbd_uuid_compare() (drbd_receiver.c) returns SPLIT_BRAIN_AUTO_RECOVER only when bitmap-UUIDs match (RULE_BITMAP_BOTH):
  4888  *rule = RULE_BITMAP_BOTH;
  4891  if (self == peer && self != ((u64)0))
  4892      return SPLIT_BRAIN_AUTO_RECOVER;
and returns SPLIT_BRAIN_DISCONNECT only when the common ancestor is found deeper, in history UUIDs (RULE_HISTORY_BOTH):
  4894  *rule = RULE_HISTORY_BOTH;
  4906  if (self == peer)
  4907      return SPLIT_BRAIN_DISCONNECT;
A matching bitmap-UUID means the on-disk bitmaps already cover the entire divergence, so a bitmap-based (incremental) resync is sufficient; only the deeper history match needs a full sync. No later code path promotes the AUTO_RECOVER USE_BITMAP result to a full sync — the only other full-sync promotion (5446-5453, --discard-my-data) is gated on the same FALSE flag.

Official DRBD docs corroborate the prose: after automatic split-brain recovery DRBD "will not initiate a full device synchronization. Instead, the automatic synchronization that now commences only covers those blocks that changed since the invocation of drbdadm --clear-bitmap new-current-uuid" (LINBIT DRBD 9.0 User Guide, Generation Identifiers / split-brain auto-recovery; corroborated via linbit.com user guide and drbd.conf-9.0(5) after-sb-* policies on manpages.debian.org). i.e. incremental/bitmap resync, not full.

CODE (byte-identical in both clones /tmp/drbdsrc/drbd/drbd_receiver.c:4395-4396 and /home/tommy/projects/drbdsrc_clone/drbd/drbd_receiver.c, inside drbd_asb_recover_1p()):
  4395  case ASB_DISCARD_SECONDARY:
  4396    return resource->role[NOW] == R_PRIMARY ? SYNC_SOURCE_USE_BITMAP : SYNC_TARGET_USE_BITMAP;
This matches the quoted line verbatim, operand order included.

SEMANTICS (drbd_receiver.c sync_descriptors[] table):
  189 [SYNC_TARGET_USE_BITMAP] = { .name = "target-use-bitmap", .is_sync_target = true, ... }
  168 [SYNC_SOURCE_USE_BITMAP] = { .name = "source-use-bitmap", .is_sync_source = true, ... }
=> SYNC_TARGET = node is overwritten (its data discarded); SYNC_SOURCE = node keeps data. So a node whose role[NOW] != R_PRIMARY (i.e. Secondary) gets SYNC_TARGET_USE_BITMAP and loses its data. Exactly the claim.

"LOSER IS SECONDARY" QUALIFIER — drbd_asb_recover_1p is only reached when exactly one node is Primary (drbd_receiver.c:5236-5245):
  5236  int pcount = (device->resource->role[NOW] == R_PRIMARY) + (peer_role == R_PRIMARY);
  5244  case 1: strategy = drbd_asb_recover_1p(peer_device); break;
resource->role[NOW] is consistently the LOCAL node's role throughout the file (e.g. 711, 5236). Per-node evaluation means the Secondary node necessarily resolves to SYNC_TARGET. Claim's "correct ONLY if the loser is Secondary, which force-secondary ensures" is sound — if both were Primary, pcount==2 routes to drbd_asb_recover_2p instead (no discard-secondary case there: line 4440 is a "Configuration error.").

OFFICIAL DOCS — Debian drbd.conf-9.0.5 manpage (manpages.debian.org/testing/drbd-utils/drbd.conf-9.0.5.en.html), verbatim: discard-secondary = "Discard the data on the secondary node." after-sb-1pri applies "with one node in primary role and one node in secondary role." NO timing/younger-primary logic. (A WebFetch summary that claimed discard-secondary can discard a Primary's data via "younger primary" timing is a model conflation with after-sb-0pri's discard-younger-primary; refuted by both the verbatim manpage and the code, which keys purely on role[NOW]==R_PRIMARY with zero timing logic.)

