The target labels (drbd_receiver.c:5724-5731) prove "connection is refused" — disconnect_rcu_unlock -> disconnect -> "change_cstate(connection, C_DISCONNECTING, CS_HARD); return -EIO;". CS_HARD forces the connection down.

HANDSHAKE-SCOPED (not just runtime adjust): the peer's after-sb values arrive in the P_PROTOCOL packet, which is sent during connection establishment. /tmp/drbdsrc/drbd/drbd_main.c:1274-1277 __drbd_send_protocol() packs "p->after_sb_0p = cpu_to_be32(nc->after_sb_0p);" (and 1p/2p); drbd_receiver.c:1078 calls "__drbd_send_protocol(connection, P_PROTOCOL)" from inside conn_connect() (the connect path, drbd_receiver.c:967). The check is gated by "if (pi->cmd != P_PROTOCOL_UPDATE)" (drbd_receiver.c:5613), so the strict-equality refusal applies to the initial P_PROTOCOL handshake.

ONE NUANCE — "identical" is true with a documented exception for the discard-local/discard-remote pair. drbd_receiver.c:5570-5582 convert_after_sb() deliberately maps them as complementary: "if (peer == ASB_DISCARD_REMOTE) return ASB_DISCARD_LOCAL;" and "if (peer == ASB_DISCARD_LOCAL) return ASB_DISCARD_REMOTE;" with comment "ASB_DISCARD_REMOTE - ASB_DISCARD_LOCAL is valid". So a node configured discard-local connects to a peer configured discard-remote (they are mirror images of the same policy). For every other after-sb-0pri value, and for all after-sb-1pri/2pri values, the comparison is plain equality (comment line 5580: "everything else is valid if they are equal on both sides"). The claim's "identical" wording is correct in spirit; the only relaxation is the local/remote mirror, which is itself a consistency requirement.

OFFICIAL CORROBORATION — drbd-user mailing list (mail-archive.com/drbd-user@lists.linbit.com/msg06662.html, thread "incompatible after-sb-0pri settings"): differing after-sb-0pri between the two nodes produced dmesg "incompatible after-sb-0pri settings", left the Primary in Standalone and the peer in WFConnection (i.e. connection did not establish); fix was to make the split-brain settings consistent on both nodes. The drbd.conf-9.0 / drbdsetup-9.0 manpages (manpages.debian.org) document after-sb-0pri/1pri/2pri as per-node net options but do not themselves state the must-match rule — that rule lives in the kernel code above, which is the authoritative source quoted here.

ATTEMPT TO REFUTE — failed. No path lets a mismatched after-sb pair connect: the only "soft" branch (P_PROTOCOL_UPDATE) skips the check but is a runtime online-reconfig packet, not the connection handshake, so it cannot be used to evade refusal at connect time. The discard-local/remote mapping is the sole non-strict-equality case and is still a cluster-wide-consistency requirement, not a loophole.
